{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Three merged; two changes bounced back with real findings\n\nMERGED: 2593 (plan_review/sizing.py split, after the tree-derived invariant fix), 2596 (jira/acli.py\nsplit), 2598 (ADR 0112). ADR 0112 is on main at 85ef5ad33ada. S0 close running.\n\n2601 (bare-repo guard) REWORKED AND RE-PUSHED as patchset 2. I caught it failing before CI did: its\nmake lint was green but tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py failed with new=1 and an extra\n'ci_gate::scripts/check_bare_repository_discovery.py' in the live set. The agent then established\nthe detector rule -- _detect_gate_scripts names gates BY SCRIPT PATH, so ANY new scripts/check_*.py\nis a new ci_gate regardless of make target or workflow step -- and resolved it well: it folded the\ndetector into tests/integration/test_bare_repository_fixture_portability_heldout.py, the file\nticket 3476 created for this exact rule and whose hand-maintained allowlist is the defect. That is\nrepair-in-place, narrower than adding a second mechanism beside it, and it explicitly rejected two\nworse options (merging into an unrelated check script; naming outside the check_*.py glob to evade\nthe detector). 31/31 on the ratchet tests, AC3 mutation re-demonstrated against the restructured\nguard. It also wrote the reasoning into the module docstring so it is not re-litigated.\n\nFiled a1f1-a30d-2d50-4f5a / celadon-computable-smelts for the underlying contradiction: AGENTS.md\nsays the ratchet 'does not forbid a new mechanism; it forbids an UNJUSTIFIED one', but the\nmechanism-ok marker only satisfies --check, while test_check_passes_on_the_committed_tree asserts\nnew=0 and test_the_committed_baseline_matches_the_live_tree asserts set(baseline)==live regardless\nof markers -- and every route to the baseline (hand-edit, --update-stale, --lock) is forbidden to a\ncontributor. So no justified mechanism is landable. Two agents hit this independently on unrelated\nbugs the same day. A secondary defect worth its own attention: make lint and the unit tests DISAGREE,\nso a contributor following the documented verification gets a false green and learns otherwise from\nCI. That is how 2601 reached Gerrit red.\n\n2600 (e2e provisioning) came back LLM-Review -1 on a substantive finding: the\npytest_collection_modifyitems hook -- the primary mechanism of the fix -- has no test coverage. The\nnine committed tests cover _toolchain.py but not the hook. On a ticket about a test harness that\nfailed silently, that is the right call. Also flagged as a likely REAL bug, not style: browser_runner\nis in _TOOLCHAIN_FIXTURES so its selection triggers collection-time provisioning, but its fixture\nbody never checks _PROVISION_ERROR/_PROVISION_ATTEMPTED -- the same asymmetric-surface gap the change\nexists to close. Sent back.\n\n2594 RECHECK FAILED AGAIN, on py3.13 and py3.11 (the first failure was py3.12 and py3.13). My\nrecheck justification asserted an intermittent race; two runs failing on three distinct Python\nversions undermines that. NOT rechecking again -- retry-until-green is the anti-pattern. Dispatched\nan investigation with an explicit stale-base hypothesis: 2594's patchset 1 predates 2593, 2596 and\n2598, all of which have since merged, so its base may simply be too old. Told the agent not to\nrecommend a recheck unless it can show the failure is provably environmental.\n\nProcess note: S0's plan attestation reads stale-code at close time, and the drift is its OWN work\nlanding -- the three file_impact paths. With currency_basis=file_impact that appears to be the\nnormal end state of any completed ticket, not a problem, since the close gate is the completion\nverifier and the claim gate was satisfied earlier. Worth confirming rather than assuming if a close\never fails on it.\n\nAlso: 'git merge --ff-only origin/main' refused in the S0 worktree because Gerrit rebases\nserver-side, so the merged commit is a different SHA than the locally pushed one. Content is\nidentical; reset --hard to origin/main in MY OWN worktree (never the shared checkout) is the clean\nresolution, and the trailer remains reachable from HEAD."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788490797264328001,"uuid":"1eea5675-d50a-4826-b20e-5f4273b422e5"}